 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T12:45+08:00",
 "answer": "Whether it is safe to cycle in Kuching this evening depends largely on your personal health sensitivity, as current air quality readings hover around the **moderate** range (with an AQI generally sitting in the 60s, though certain localized or fine particulate metrics can fluctuate). \n\nHere is what you should consider before heading out:\n\n* **For most healthy adults:** Moderate air quality means the air is generally acceptable. A casual or short evening ride is usually fine, but you might want to avoid heavy, high-intensity exertion (like fast-paced training or long-distance cycling) which forces you to breathe deeply and take in more pollutants.\n* **For sensitive groups:** If you have asthma, pre-existing respiratory conditions, heart issues, or if you are pregnant or elderly, it is **best to avoid outdoor exercising** during hazy conditions. Fine particulate matter ($PM_{2.5}$) can cause throat irritation, coughing, or shortness of breath even at moderate levels.\n\n**Recommendations if you decide to ride:**\n* Check the real-time Air Pollutant Index (API) via the Department of Environment Malaysia (Jas/APIMS) or local monitoring apps just before you head out, as haze conditions can shift quickly.\n* Keep your ride short and moderate in intensity.\n* If you start to experience any coughing, throat irritation, or chest discomfort, cut your ride short and head indoors.",
